00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:55.950
Now you may wonder, what are
the most important things

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:58.890
to take note of for cost
as well as performance?

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:00.750
In this section,
we'll be covering

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:03.510
the best practices
for cost optimization

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:05.760
as well as performance
optimization.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:08.460
I'll be putting some links in
the description box below as

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:10.380
well for your further reading.

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:12.910
To optimize performance,
first of all,

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:14.940
remember the specified
minimum number

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:17.220
of container instances
you want to be

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:19.650
kept warm and ready
to serve requests.

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:22.080
Secondly, don't
forget to also try

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:24.210
to minimize the number
of dependencies,

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:27.300
as well as the size of them, to
make sure that your service is

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:28.290
a lean one.

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:32.280
Thirdly, lazily load code which
is infrequently being used

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:33.960
if your language supports it.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:37.860
And also make use of
global variables, as well.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:40.500
The initialization
of global variables

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:43.440
occurs during the start
up, which can severely

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:45.420
increase the code start time.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:48.270
Now, one great
example of a variable

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:50.460
that is expensive
to initialize will

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:52.140
be the database connections.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:56.740
Database connections are great
to keep as global variables.

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:00.660
And finally, make use of
minimal container images.

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:03.930
Larger container images
can increase your security

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:04.980
vulnerabilities.

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:09.360
And this is because they contain
more than what your code needs.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:12.420
As for cost optimization,
setting the right number

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:16.290
of maximum instances, depending
on your traffic as well as

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:18.720
desired level of
request latency,

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.610
can help you to optimize
your overall costs.

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:25.170
Secondly, it is also important
to take note and optimize

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:27.570
your CPU as well as RAM.

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:30.570
CPU usage is generally
a huge contributor

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:32.670
to what your overall costs.

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:35.100
Thirdly, it is also
highly important

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:38.940
to make use of billing
alerts, as well as labels.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:41.670
Budget alerts provide
an early warning signal

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:44.100
to unexpected
increases in your bill.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:47.790
And as for labels, labels allow
you to assign a simple text

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:50.040
value to particular resources.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:51.990
And this allows
you to understand

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:54.360
which are the most
significant contributors

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:57.510
towards your overall bill
to allow you to take action

00:09:57.510 --> 00:09:58.740
on them.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:02.340
On top of that, implementing
basic local caching,

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:07.110
where a local in-memory copy of
frequently loaded or generated

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:11.130
data is being kept, can produce
significant and dramatic

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:14.400
improvements to both cost
as well as performance.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.770
And last but not
least, do not forget

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:19.560
to always make sure you
measure the effectiveness

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:22.410
of your changes so that
you can keep track of them

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:25.710
and continue to improve
and optimize the costs.

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:27.930
Now, let's take a look
at a fictitious use

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:30.900
case of a simple
e-commerce application.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:32.610
When an order is
being first placed

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:35.430
on the front end, which is
being deployed on Cloud Run,

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:37.920
Cloud Run receives the
request and sends it

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:41.940
